From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lucascouts <lucascs@protonmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:52:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent: tag threads, show tag chips and filter by #tag
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

0048's store has held tags since story 031, with add_tag, remove_tag and
a per-thread key list, and nothing in the interface wrote or showed one.
This patch is that interface, the third consumer of the store after 0049
and 0065: story 037, 2026-10-20.

What it does. A thread row's context menu, in a project group or in the
Pinned section, offers "Tags…" right after "Pin Thread"/"Unpin Thread".
It opens a keyboard picker over every existing tag, the thread's own
marked as applied: typing narrows the list by key, a valid new tag is
offered first as "Add tag “<text>”", Enter toggles a tag and keeps the
picker open on the confirmed entry, Escape closes it and keeps what was
confirmed. A tagged row shows its tags as chips after the title, ordered
by key, three at most plus "+N", with a tooltip naming them all; a row
with no tag draws as before. `#tag` terms in the sidebar filter keep only
the threads carrying every named tag (AND), in groups and in the Pinned
section, combined with the rest of the text as typed; History accepts the
same terms. With no `#` term both filters behave exactly as before.

How. One parser, agent_ui::thread_tag_filter::parse_tag_query, serves
both filters; it never lowercases or re-joins the text, so the sidebar
keeps matching the raw query and History lowercases its own, after
parsing (U+0130 lowercases to two scalars, which would make a valid
32-character tag read as invalid). The raw query, `#` terms included,
stays what every existing is_empty() check reads, so a tag term loads and
expands collapsed groups and the Pinned section and turns drag off, as
search text does. The picker is agent_ui::thread_tags_picker; its Picker
owns the 24 rem width (initial_width), so every row, the selection
highlight and the separators are laid out inside the modal frame -- a
fixed-width wrapper around an unsized Picker let them overrun it (R1.13).
The sidebar side is crates/sidebar/src/thread_tags.rs. ThreadItem
(crates/ui) gains badges -- several, each with its own element id and
tooltip, in builder call order -- and tag chips after them; a row with
either ends a long title in an ellipsis instead of under the fade.

Failure: a refused add or removal leaves the thread's tags unchanged,
shows the refusal in the picker and logs one warning naming the thread id
and the refusal, never a title or tag text. Without the store (try_global
is None) no "Tags…" entry, no chip and no tag filtering are offered; a
missing workspace logs a warning and opens nothing. A row a tag term hides
keeps its notification and access time, so removing the term brings both
back.

Upstream touch points. crates/sidebar/src/sidebar.rs only through hooks
a-h, 7 hunks and 28 added lines, none removed: (a) parse_tag_query after
`let query = ...`; (b) retain_tagged before the group's
`if !query.is_empty()` branch; (c) the text part as the branch's query;
(d, e) the tag-only kept threads and a `continue` for a label match with
no tagged thread, both before the unchanged emptiness check; (f)
retain_tagged_rows before the unchanged filter_pinned_rows line; (g)
tags_menu_entry after "Pin Thread"; (h) .tags(tag_chips_for(..)) in the row
builder; plus the module lines. pinned_section.rs: filter_pinned_rows'
first statements strip the `#` terms. threads_archive_view.rs: a store
observation in new() and the tag filter in update_items. agent_ui.rs: two
`pub mod` lines. thread_organization: `pub(crate) mod test_helpers`,
`pub(crate) fn capture_logs` and `pub(crate) fn all_lines_lowercased`,
because the picker's refusal test needs 0048's log capture and a test
binary can install one logger only. The lines 0063 rewrites
(should_load_threads, the emptiness check, `if is_collapsed {`,
filter_pinned_rows' call, the Pinned expand check) stay byte-identical.

0063 is regenerated on top with three changes and no other: (1) its
ThreadItem badge field, builder, rendering and thread_item_badge_tests.rs
move here, generalised to several badges; its
badge(IconName::ToolWeb, "Devtools thread") call is unchanged; (2) its
three test_helpers visibility hunks move here; (3) a test-only hunk in
sidebar_devtools_tests.rs guards the composition of its devtools-only
view with the tag filter -- no code hunk, since its devtools-only retain
already runs before retain_tagged for groups, collapsed groups and pinned
rows. Nineteen more are regenerated with no other change than context,
offsets and index lines: 0010, 0015, 0027-0030, 0035, 0037, 0039, 0040,
0043-0045, 0052, 0054, 0056-0058 and 0067 -- most of them only for the
two `pub mod` lines in agent_ui.rs's module list.

Tests: 77, in eleven new files -- ui thread_item_badge_tests (15), agent_ui
thread_tag_filter_tests (9), thread_tags_picker_tests (10),
thread_tags_picker_selection_tests (4), thread_tags_picker_unicode_tests
(2), thread_tags_picker_layout_tests (3), threads_archive_view_tags_tests
(6), threads_archive_view_unicode_tests (3), sidebar sidebar_tags_tests
(6), sidebar_tags_filter_tests (14), sidebar_tags_notify_tests (5).

UNGROUPED and unconditional, right after 0065: it consumes 0048's store,
0049's Pinned section and 0065's filter guard on drag, and needs no
adapter. The claude-agent-acp-plus patches that also edit these files
(0015 and 0028-0030 among them), 0063 and 0067 are generated on top of
it.

a5c4ca8b review (2026-10-25, story 061): upstream's threads-db index is not a
numbered migration: ThreadsDatabase::new now runs `CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS
threads_list_metadata ON threads(...)`, a covering index for its listing query,
in the separate threads database. No patch touches crates/agent/src/db.rs. This
patch adds no storage of its own; it reads 0048's ThreadOrganizationDb domain;
sqlez keys migrations per domain, so nothing collides with upstream's numbering,
and the `threads` table and the index's columns are untouched. No code change
needed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
